QA2 = [
# ---------- Section 4: Catalog & inventory ----------
(4, "What operations does the catalog API expose?",
 "Branch-scoped categories (list, create), products (list, create, restock, patch, delete), and product lookup for the admin app. Every route resolves the caller's branch scope first, so a manager cannot read or mutate another branch's catalog even with a valid token.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts"),
(4, "How are duplicate category names handled?",
 "The database enforces uniqueness of category name per branch among non-deleted rows; the route returns a conflict error instead of silently creating a second 'Drinks'. Because offline tills can race, the constraint is the real guard — optimistic UI checks are advisory only.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (category); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts"),
(4, "Why can't two products share an SKU in a branch?",
 "An SKU is the barcode-level identity a cashier or scanner types; duplicates would make stock movements ambiguous. Uniqueness is per branch, not global, so two shops can stock the same manufacturer SKU. The server rejects duplicates; the POS surfaces the error on the next sync.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (product); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts; backend/test/catalog.test.ts"),
(4, "How does restock work?",
 "POST restock increments stock_quantity rather than setting it, stamped with a new server_version so tills receive the change on their next pull. Incrementing avoids lost updates when the admin restocks while a sale is being applied on another till.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (restock); backend/src/routes/sync.ts"),
(4, "What happens when an admin edits a product price?",
 "The product row updates price_cents and gets a new server_version; POS pulls the new row. Already-printed sales are unaffected because sale_item snapshots the price sold. If a till pushes a sale using an old price, the server accepts the sale but returns a PRICE_DRIFT warning so the discrepancy is visible.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts; backend/src/routes/sync.ts (warnings)"),
(4, "What distinguishes is_active from deleted_at?",
 "is_active hides something from the till's sellable list while keeping it queryable (for example, temporarily unavailable), whereas deleted_at is the tombstone that marks the item removed and must replicate to all tills. Both are synced; only deletion removes the item from offline tables.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql; orangemain/.../data/repository/ProductRepository.kt"),
(4, "Why is branch deletion blocked when a device is active?",
 "A branch with a live till cannot be removed without corrupting that device's scope: its queued sales would have nowhere valid to land. The route checks for non-revoked devices and refuses, forcing the admin to revoke/retire tills first — an explicit, auditable sequence.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (branch delete); backend/migrations/001_init.sql (device)"),
(4, "What is the known category-deletion race?",
 "Two clients can try to delete or recreate the same category name concurrently; because soft-deleted rows still exist in the table, a partially-applied unique index can reject a legitimate recreation. It is documented as an open hardening item — the practical fix is a partial unique index excluding deleted rows plus a retry.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql; CHANGELOG.md §8 (open items)"),
(4, "Where is the known N+1 query problem?",
 "Branch and product listing code path assembles per-branch summaries with repeated queries in a loop instead of one aggregate join. With the current data sizes it is fine, but it is logged as an open optimisation so the team is not surprised when the catalog grows.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts; CHANGELOG.md §8"),
(4, "How is admin inventory input validated?",
 "Client-side, InventoryInput parses quantity and price text into integers with bounds before calling the API; a unit test covers edge cases. Server-side, zod rejects negative quantities and malformed cents. Two layers because the admin app is the human interface to money and stock.",
 "orangeadmin/app/src/main/java/com/sixteenhalves/orangeadmin/utils/InventoryInput.kt; orangeadmin/app/src/test/.../InventoryInputTest.kt; backend/src/routes/catalog.ts"),
(4, "How are sales listed in the admin app?",
 "GET sales per branch uses keyset pagination — limit plus an opaque cursor over (created_at, id) — returning next_cursor. Keyset avoids the deep-offset slowdown and stays stable while new sales arrive, which matters because a busy till can create rows during scrolling.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §2 (pagination); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (sales list); orangeadmin/.../repository/AdminRepository.kt"),
(4, "Which catalog fields does the POS pull?",
 "id, category, name, SKU, price_cents, stock_quantity and flags, with tombstones for deletions. The POS stores them locally and rebuilds its sellable grid; totals are computed locally in cents and validated again server-side on push. Catalog changes flow one way: admin edits, POS consumes.",
 "orangemain/.../data/model/Product.kt; .../data/repository/ProductRepository.kt; backend/src/routes/sync.ts (pull)"),
(4, "Who owns the tax rate and where is it applied?",
 "The POS computes tax locally using a default rate of 1600 basis points (16% Kenyan VAT) and stores subtotal, tax and total in cents on the sale. The server validates the arithmetic envelope rather than recomputing a business policy it cannot see, so tax changes ship with the app configuration.",
 "orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt (DEFAULT_TAX_RATE_BP = 1600)"),
(4, "How does the admin add a new product from a branch screen?",
 "AddInventoryItemScreen/BranchAddInventoryScreen collect name, category, price and initial stock, call the create-product endpoint, and refresh the branch detail snapshot. On success the server assigns id and server_version; on failure the UI shows the stable error code's message and keeps the form data.",
 "orangeadmin/app/src/main/java/com/sixteenhalves/orangeadmin/screens/main/AddInventoryItemScreen.kt; BranchAddInventoryScreen.kt; .../repository/AdminRepository.kt"),

# ---------- Section 5: Device provisioning ----------
(5, "How is a POS till provisioned?",
 "An admin opens the branch's device screen, names a new till, and the server issues device_id, device_code and a one-time device_token (201). The admin shows that credential to the till via QR or copy text; the till stores it locally and uses it for sync. The token is never retrievable again.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (POST /branches/:id/devices); orangeadmin/.../screens/main/BranchDeviceScreen.kt"),
(5, "What is in the device credential?",
 "The share text carries the backend URL, the device id, the device code, and the token — the four values the POS needs to talk to the API. DeviceCredentials renders it as a compact block, and parseDeviceCredentials accepts it back in several shapes (URL query, key: value lines, key=value).",
 "orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/util/DeviceCredentials.kt"),
(5, "How does the QR transfer work?",
 "The admin app renders the share text as a QR bitmap in QrCodeImage, shown on the device screen. The cashier scans it with the tablet camera during setup; the raw string goes through the same parser used for pasted text, so a scan and a paste cannot diverge.",
 "orangeadmin/.../components/shared/QrCodeImage.kt; .../screens/main/BranchDeviceScreen.kt; orangeadmin/app/src/test/.../QrCodeImageTest.kt; DeviceShareTextTest.kt"),
(5, "What does the POS accept on paste?",
 "parseDeviceCredentials trims the input, strips a URI prefix if present, then reads key/value pairs separated by ':' or '=' on each line, percent-decoding values and normalising keys (case and punctuation-insensitive). Missing required fields return null, so setup shows a validation error instead of saving half a credential.",
 "orangemain/.../util/DeviceCredentials.kt; .../ui/screens (Setup screens); orangemain/shared/src/commonTest"),
(5, "Why is the token returned only once?",
 "Returning a secret repeatedly would make it recoverable by anyone with admin tokens, broadening exposure. One-time issuance plus hashed storage means the admin must re-provision a till if the credential is lost — a deliberate friction that keeps device identity trustworthy.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (token issuance, sha256Hex); backend/migrations/001_init.sql (token_hash)"),
(5, "What is a device heartbeat?",
 "A lightweight POST /devices/heartbeat with device_id, outbox_pending and app_version, authenticated with the device token. It updates last_seen_at and app version; an unprovisioned till simply skips it. The POS calls it every sync cycle.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (heartbeat); backend/migrations/002_...sql (app_version); orangemain/.../data/remote/BackendApi.kt (heartbeat)"),
(5, "How does the admin know a till is alive?",
 "The device list shows last_seen_at from heartbeats, flags 'never seen' devices, and highlights stale ones (no heartbeat for more than 24 hours). Pending-outbox depth rides along on the heartbeat as a fleet diagnostic, so a connected-but-stuck till is visible.",
 "orangeadmin/.../screens/main/BranchDeviceScreen.kt; .../domain/AdminData.kt (device fields); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (heartbeat)"),
(5, "What happens when a device is revoked?",
 "The row gets is_revoked, and every device-authenticated request from that token fails, so the till can no longer push or pull. Queued sales stay locally and need re-provisioning or manual transfer. Revocation is reversible in the sense that the record remains for audit.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (revoke); backend/migrations/001_init.sql (is_revoked); backend/test/catalog.test.ts"),
(5, "Why is there a separate permanent delete?",
 "Retiring a lost or dead till should eventually remove its row, but only after revocation has cut access. The permanent-delete endpoint hard-deletes the device record, leaving sales' device references as historical identifiers. Two explicit steps beat a single ambiguous 'delete'.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (DELETE /devices/:device_id/permanent)"),
(5, "How does the POS know which backend to call?",
 "The backend URL is part of the device credential, saved to local settings. BackendApi builds requests from DeviceSettings.root(), trimming trailing slashes, so a till can point at a staging or production host without a rebuild. The admin app, by contrast, has its base URL compiled into NetworkModule.",
 "orangemain/.../data/remote/BackendApi.kt (configured/root); .../data/repository/SettingsRepository.kt; orangeadmin/.../di/NetworkModule.kt"),
(5, "Why use a human-readable device_code as well as a UUID?",
 "The code is what a person reads off a screen during support calls ('till 2 at Kibera'), while the UUID is the stable key. Logs and audit rows can carry the code without exposing internal identifiers, and the admin UI can label devices meaningfully.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts; backend/migrations/001_init.sql (device_code)"),
(5, "How is a lost till handled?",
 "Revoke it first — all sync stops immediately, so no more sales can be injected. Then provision a replacement: a new row, new token, new code. The lost device's queued sales are lost unless exportable; that trade-off is explicit because the alternative is trusting a stolen credential.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (revoke + provision); backend/migrations/001_init.sql"),
(5, "What happens if a till is unprovisioned?",
 "SyncService still runs but skips authenticated calls: no heartbeat, no push/pull. The UI directs the cashier to setup, where a QR or pasted credential completes provisioning. This avoids error loops from a device token that does not exist yet.",
 "orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt (unprovisioned till skips heartbeat); .../ui/screens (setup)"),
(5, "Will a revoked till keep syncing in the background?",
 "No. The token check happens server-side on every push/pull/heartbeat, so revocation is effective at the next request regardless of what the app believes. The app may show a generic auth failure until the admin tells the cashier to re-provision, which is acceptable for v1.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (device auth); backend/src/routes/sync.ts"),

# ---------- Section 6: Sync engine ----------
(6, "What does POST /sync/push accept?",
 "A batch containing products, categories and sales, each with ids and client updated_at timestamps, capped at 200 records and 5 MB. The server processes records individually in savepoints so one bad row does not roll back the whole batch, and returns per-record outcomes plus warnings.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §2"),
(6, "What does GET /sync/pull return?",
 "Rows changed since the caller's cursor (last server_version seen), paged with limit/cursor, including tombstones. Each row carries its server_version; the device advances its cursor after applying a page. The POS caps pages per cycle to keep each sync short.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts; orangemain/.../data/remote/BackendApi.kt (pull); .../data/sync/SyncService.kt (MAX_PULL_PAGES)"),
(6, "What is the conflict resolution rule?",
 "Last-write-wins on updated_at: if the incoming record's timestamp is newer than the stored row's, the write is applied; otherwise the server keeps the stored row and reports the conflict. There is no field-level merge — the whole record wins or loses, which is easy to reason about under intermittent connectivity.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (upsert handlers, drifted/compare logic)"),
(6, "How are ties broken?",
 "An incoming timestamp equal to the stored one does not overwrite the stored row; it is treated as not-strictly-newer and logged as a stale/duplicate write. Deterministic tie-breaking matters because offline devices often push the same record twice after a failed acknowledgement.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (recordStaleWrite, compare)"),
(6, "What is the clock-skew guard?",
 "Any incoming record stamped more than 5 minutes ahead of server time is rejected with CLOCK_AHEAD; malformed timestamps get INVALID_TIMESTAMP. Without this, one till with a wildly wrong clock would win every last-write-wins conflict forever, silently erasing admin edits.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (clockSkewError, MAX_CLOCK_AHEAD)"),
(6, "What happens to a write that loses LWW?",
 "It is not applied, and the server writes an audit row SYNC_STALE_WRITE containing the device code, the incoming and stored timestamps, and the record id. This makes dropped offline writes observable — an operator can reconstruct what the till thought it had.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (recordStaleWrite); backend/src/lib/audit.ts"),
(6, "Why per-record savepoints?",
 "A batch mixes many records; one constraint violation (missing foreign key, bad category) must not discard the valid rest. Each record is applied inside a SAVEPOINT that rolls back alone on failure, and the response reports which records failed and why.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (inSavepoint, applySale)"),
(6, "How are sales made idempotent on push?",
 "The sale id is client-generated and is the primary key; re-pushing the same sale collides instead of duplicating. Receipt number uniqueness adds a second guard. The apply routine runs once and stock movements ride the same transaction.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (sale insert); backend/migrations/001_init.sql (sale pk, receipt unique)"),
(6, "What does sync return besides success?",
 "Per-record versions for the device to update, a payment status for M-Pesa sales forced to PENDING, and warnings: FK_MISSING when a line references an unknown product, PRICE_DRIFT when the sold price differs from the catalog. The till can surface or queue these without failing the sale.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (applySale return, warnings)"),
(6, "Why does the server force M-Pesa sales to PENDING?",
 "A POS could claim paid after receiving nothing. Payment state is server-owned for M-Pesa: the push sets payment_status PENDING and nulls any client-supplied receipt. Only the Daraja callback or reconciliation can mark it PAID, which makes the money trail independent of the client's claims.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (paymentStatus computation)"),
(6, "What are the batch and page limits and why?",
 "Push accepts up to 200 records / 5 MB, and the POS pulls at most 20 pages per cycle with 200 products and 25 sales per batch. The asymmetry protects the free-tier server and keeps each sync short enough to complete on a flaky connection rather than timing out halfway.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §0; orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt (MAX_* constants)"),
(6, "How often does the POS sync?",
 "App.kt runs a loop: perform a sync cycle, then delay 60 seconds. Cashier actions can also trigger an immediate cycle (for example after checkout), so the latency to the admin dashboard is seconds even though the heartbeat cadence is a minute.",
 "orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/App.kt (LaunchedEffect, delay(60_000)); SyncService.syncCycle"),
(6, "What happens when a sync cycle fails?",
 "Each phase is wrapped in runCatching so a network failure does not crash the app or lose the queue; unsent records stay pending and are retried next cycle. The heartbeat is attempted independently, so the admin still learns the till is alive even if a push fails.",
 "orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt (runCatching around heartbeat and phases)"),
(6, "How are products created offline on the POS?",
 "The POS mints a client id, stores the row locally with its own updated_at, and pushes it in the next batch. LWW then decides against any concurrent admin edit. This is why ids are application-generated UUIDs rather than database serials.",
 "orangemain/.../data/repository/ProductRepository.kt; backend/src/routes/sync.ts (product upsert); backend/src/lib/ids.ts"),
(6, "What is the main limitation of LWW here?",
 "It loses concurrent edits to different fields when they happen within the same record: a device updating stock and an admin updating price on the same product will keep only the later record. For a v1 retail system this is accepted and made visible through audit; field-level merge is future work.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts; CHANGELOG.md §8"),
(6, "Why not use CRDTs or operational transforms?",
 "The domain is mostly append-only (sales) plus simple catalog edits, where LWW's cost — rare lost edits, observable in audit — is far below the cost of CRDT infrastructure and developer complexity. Choosing LWW was a deliberate scope decision, not ignorance of alternatives.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts; project-description-revised.md"),
(6, "How does the pull cursor survive server restarts?",
 "server_version comes from a PostgreSQL sequence, which persists in the database. A device cursor is just the last value it saw, so restarts and redeploys change nothing; gaps from rolled-back savepoints are fine because the cursor is a lower bound.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (server_version_seq); backend/src/routes/sync.ts"),
(6, "What guarantees that a till never misses a row?",
 "Two rules: rows only become visible with a server_version greater than the device's cursor, and the cursor advances only after a page is applied. If application half-fails, the device re-pulls the same page — applying it again is harmless because upserts are keyed by id.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (pull paging); orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt"),
(6, "How does the POS handle a product deleted while offline?",
 "The pull returns the tombstone with a newer server_version; the local row is removed (or flagged) and the sellable list refreshes. If the till already queued a sale containing that product, the server still applies the sale but returns FK_MISSING/PRICE_DRIFT-style warnings for review.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (tombstones, warnings); orangemain/.../data/repository/ProductRepository.kt"),
(6, "How is the outbox represented?",
 "Locally-pending records (sales, catalog edits) live in the SQLDelight database with a pending flag; the outbox depth is simply the count of unsent sales, which is also what the heartbeat reports. There is no separate queue file to corrupt.",
 "orangemain/.../data/local/DatabaseDriverFactory.kt; .../data/repository/TransactionRepository.kt; SyncService.heartbeat"),
(6, "What is a typical sync cycle order?",
 "Heartbeat first (cheap liveness), then push pending local records, then pull pages until caught up, then persist the new cursor. Receipts and stock are already correct locally, so ordering only affects how fast the admin sees data — never correctness of the till's own ledger.",
 "orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt (syncCycle); .../data/remote/BackendApi.kt"),
(6, "How do sales get their receipt numbers?",
 "The till generates the receipt number at sale time so it can print immediately while offline; uniqueness is enforced server-side on sale.receipt_number. A duplicate receipt is rejected on push, which protects against two tills accidentally minting the same sequence.",
 "orangemain/.../data/repository/TransactionRepository.kt; backend/migrations/001_init.sql (receipt unique); backend/src/routes/sync.ts"),
(6, "What happens to stock after a sale syncs?",
 "The server decrements product stock per line inside the sale's savepoint. Concurrent sales on the same product are serialised by the database row update, so the final quantity reflects every committed sale; the admin dashboard's low-stock count is a query over the result.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (applySale stock update); backend/src/routes/stats.ts"),
(6, "Why store client created_at and server received_at separately?",
 "created_at is business time (when the cashier rang the sale), used for reporting; received_at is when the server learned about it, used for debugging lag and for the clock-skew guard. Conflating them would make delayed syncs look like time travel.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (sale.received_at); backend/src/routes/sync.ts"),
(6, "How was sync hardened this round?",
 "Two changes: a 5-minute-ahead clock guard that rejects absurd timestamps, and an audit record for every write that lost LWW, including tombstones and equal-timestamp pushes. Tests cover both, and the suite passes 35/35.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts; backend/test/sync.test.ts; CHANGELOG.md §3.4"),

# ---------- Section 7: Payments (first part) ----------
(7, "Explain the M-Pesa STK flow end to end.",
 "The POS rings an M-Pesa sale (stored locally as PENDING) and syncs it. The admin/POS triggers STK push; the server validates the sale and amount, checks for an in-flight attempt, normalises the phone to E.164, calls Daraja's STK API and stores a PENDING payment_transaction. The customer enters their PIN; Daraja calls our callback; on success the server marks the transaction and sale PAID with the receipt. The POS polls payment status and prints a receipt when paid.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts; backend/src/payments/daraja.ts; orangemain/.../data/remote/BackendApi.kt (stkPush)"),
(7, "How are phone numbers normalised?",
 "normalizeMsisdn accepts 07XX..., 2547XX... and +2547XX... and returns strict E.164 (+2547...); anything else fails with INVALID_MSISDN 422. Daraja is picky about format, and normalising server-side means a cashier typo is caught before an unsuccessful prompt confuses the customer.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (normalizeMsisdn, INVALID_MSISDN)"),
(7, "Why validate the STK amount against the sale total?",
 "The request includes an amount; the server recomputes the sale total and rejects mismatches with AMOUNT_MISMATCH 422. This blocks both bugs (stale cart totals) and fraud (a client asking to charge less than the recorded sale). The amount sent to Daraja is the server's figure.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (AMOUNT_MISMATCH)"),
(7, "How are duplicate STK prompts prevented?",
 "Before calling Daraja the server checks for a PENDING attempt on the same sale and returns STK_IN_PROGRESS 409. A race between two requests is closed by re-checking under the transaction/insert path, so a double-tap cannot send two prompts to the customer's phone.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (STK_IN_PROGRESS)"),
(7, "What is the STK timeout sweeper?",
 "PENDING attempts older than the configured timeout are marked FAILED, the sale stays unpaid, and an audit event payments.stk_timeout is written. The sweep runs as part of payment-status handling and before new attempts, so a lost callback cannot leave a sale pending forever.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (sweeper, stk-timeout audit)"),
(7, "How is the Daraja callback protected?",
 "A shared secret must match either the X-Callback-Secret header or the ?secret= query parameter; otherwise 401 CALLBACK_UNAUTHORIZED. This is a pragmatic replacement for IP allow-listing, which Daraja's sandbox does not provide. The callback then always returns 200 after authentication so Daraja does not retry-storm on parse issues we can fix later.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (callback auth); backend/src/config.ts (callback URL secret)"),
(7, "Why log raw callbacks before parsing?",
 "daraja_callback stores the verbatim body first, then the handler parses. If Daraja changes a field or sends something unexpected, the evidence is already durable; the payload reference daraja_callback:<id> links the audit trail back to exactly what arrived.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (daraja_callback insert)"),
(7, "What happens on an unknown checkout id?",
 "The callback is logged and the handler returns without touching payment state — there is no matching payment_transaction, so acting would be guessing. It sits in the raw log for investigation; the reconciliation screen covers cases where a transaction exists but disagrees.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (unknown attempt branch)"),
(7, "How are duplicate callbacks handled?",
 "Processing is keyed by the payment_transaction's state: a success callback for an already-PAID transaction is a no-op, and the unique reconciliation index prevents duplicate queue rows. Idempotent callback handling is essential because Daraja retries deliveries.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts; backend/migrations/002_...sql (reconciliation unique index)"),
(7, "What does a successful callback change?",
 "It marks payment_transaction SUCCESS with the receipt number, sets the sale to PAID and records the M-Pesa receipt, and clears the sale from any reconciliation need. Amount checks run before the update, so a short-paid callback is flagged rather than trusted.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (callback success path)"),
(7, "What if success arrives with no receipt?",
 "That is not silently trusted: the case is queued for reconciliation so a human can verify with the M-Pesa statement. Missing receipts usually mean a partial or malformed callback, and auto-marking such a sale paid would break the ledger's provability.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts; backend/src/routes/reconciliation.ts"),
]
